    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d475">

                <pb facs="0646" n="550" sameAs="frus:frus1921v02/pg550"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.6363/51: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in the Netherlands</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Phillips</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-04-26T17:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">April 26,
                                1921—5 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>27. Legation’s no. 46 of April 19, noon, and no. 49 of April 25, 6 p.m.<anchor
                        n="49" xml:id="d475-f1"/> An amendment to the Philippine petroleum law was
                    recently passed by the Philippine Legislature. The amendment provides that 61
                    percent of the stock of a leasing company must be <pb facs="0647" n="551"
                        xml:id="pg551"/> held by citizens either of the Philippine Islands or of the
                    United States. This measure is not in accord with the provisions for reciprocity
                    contained in the United States general leasing law of February 25, 1920. You are
                    authorized to state positively that the American Government will take steps to
                    secure from the Philippine Legislature at its October session the passage of a
                    law which will be in conformity with the reciprocity provisions of the general
                    leasing law of the United States.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
